    "genre": "Paper 02 Question 1 · SOs 1.2, 1.3, 1.4, 1.5, 1.6, 1.9, 1.13, 1.14, 1.12 (20 marks)",
    "stimulus": "<p><b>Island Pulse Radio</b> is a small radio station on Old Street, Roseau. Listeners send song requests on a web form and by WhatsApp. The DJ, Jahmal, plays music from a laptop and keeps the station's 9,000 songs (about 45 GB) on an old external hard drive. Last month the drive failed during a live show and the station was off air for two hours.</p><p>The manager, Mrs. Baron-Lake, has a budget of $1,500 to buy one new computer for the studio and wants a better way to store and back up the music library. She has asked you, a Form 5 IT student on work experience, to advise her.</p>",
    "prompt": "<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>A listener types a song request on the station's web form; the request appears on Jahmal's screen in a queue, the queue is saved, and the song plays on air. Using this example, <b>describe</b> each stage of the input–process–output–storage (IPOS) cycle. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Two computers are offered for the studio:<br><b>Computer P:</b> desktop, Intel Core i7 3.4 GHz (8 cores), 16 GB RAM, 1 TB SSD, 6 USB ports, $1,350.<br><b>Computer Q:</b> tablet, 2.0 GHz quad-core, 4 GB RAM, 64 GB flash storage, 1 USB-C port, $600.<br><b>(i)</b> State which computer you would recommend. <i>(1 mark)</i><br><b>(ii)</b> Justify your choice by referring to THREE of the specifications. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>The music library is 45 GB. <b>(i)</b> Convert 45 GB to megabytes, showing your working. <i>(1 mark)</i> <b>(ii)</b> <b>Discuss</b> ONE advantage and ONE disadvantage of storing the library in the cloud rather than on a local drive at the station. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>The request form has the fields <b>ListenerName</b>, <b>Phone</b> (7 digits), <b>Age</b> and <b>SongTitle</b>. <b>(i)</b> Name and describe a suitable validation check for Phone and a different one for Age. <i>(2 marks)</i> <b>(ii)</b> <b>Distinguish</b> between validation and verification. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>A post on social media says Island Pulse Radio is \"closing down for good\". It has no author, no date and comes from an account created yesterday. <b>Explain</b> how a listener could use TWO criteria to decide whether the post is reliable. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>",
