    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Input:</b> the listener types the song title and name into the web form and presses Send. <b>Processing:</b> the system checks the request and places it in order in the request queue. <b>Output:</b> the queue is displayed on Jahmal's screen and the song is played on air through the speakers/transmitter. <b>Storage:</b> the request queue (and a log of songs played) is saved on the computer's drive for later use.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> Computer P. <b>(ii)</b> Its Core i7 3.4 GHz 8-core processor can run audio-mixing and streaming software at the same time without lag. Its 16 GB of RAM lets large audio files and several programs stay in memory, so playback does not stutter on air. Its 1 TB SSD can hold the 45 GB library more than 20 times over and is fast and shock-resistant, and its six USB ports connect the mixing desk, microphones and backup drive. At $1,350 it is within the $1,500 budget. Computer Q's 64 GB storage could not hold the library and backups, and its single port is too few.</p><p><b>(c)(i)</b> 45 × 1,024 = <b>46,080 MB</b>. <b>(ii)</b> Advantage: a cloud copy is stored off-site, so if the studio drive fails again (or the building is damaged in a hurricane) the library can be restored and even played from another location. Disadvantage: the station depends on its Internet connection; if WaitukubuliTel's service is down, files cannot be reached, and large uploads/downloads are slow and there is a monthly subscription cost.</p><p><b>(d)(i)</b> Phone: a <b>length check</b> rejects any entry that is not exactly 7 characters (a type check could also allow digits only). Age: a <b>range check</b> accepts only ages between, for example, 10 and 100. <b>(ii)</b> <b>Validation</b> is an automatic check by the computer that data is sensible and follows set rules, but it cannot tell if the data is true. <b>Verification</b> checks that data has been entered or copied accurately from its source, for example by double entry or by proofreading against the original.</p><p><b>(e)</b> <b>Authority:</b> the post has no named author and comes from an account created yesterday, so the listener cannot tell who wrote it or whether they have any link to the station. They should check the station's official website or verified page. <b>Currency:</b> the post has no date, so even if a closure was once discussed, the information may be out of date. <b>Accuracy</b> could also be checked by comparing it with The Roseau Courier or the station's own announcements. The post fails these tests, so it is not reliable.</p>",
    "key": "<p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) 1 mark for each IPOS stage correctly applied to the scenario (4). A stage named without the example: max 2 overall.</li><li>(b)(i) Computer P (1). (ii) 1 mark each for THREE specifications linked to a studio need: processor/cores, RAM, SSD capacity/speed, ports, price within budget (3). Choosing Q earns 0 for (i) but a justification that correctly compares specs may earn 1.</li><li>(c)(i) 46,080 MB with working (1); accept 45,000 MB only if 1 GB = 1,000 MB is stated. (ii) Advantage (1), disadvantage (1), development linked to the station (1).</li><li>(d)(i) Suitable check for Phone (length/type/format) (1); different suitable check for Age (range/type) (1). (ii) Validation definition (1); verification definition (1).</li><li>(e) 1 mark for each criterion named, 1 mark for each applied to the post (4).</li></ul>",
    "teacherTip": "<p>Candidates often describe IPOS in general terms and forget the scenario: always say <i>what</i> is input, processed, output and stored here. In (b), \"it is faster\" earns nothing; quote the specification and say what it does for the user. Many confuse validation and verification: validation = sensible, verification = copied correctly.</p>",
