     "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Input:</b> the listener types the song title and name into the web form and presses Send. <b>Processing:</b> the system checks the request and places it in order in the request queue. <b>Output:</b> the queue is displayed on Jahmal's screen and the song is played on air through the speakers/transmitter. <b>Storage:</b> the request queue (and a log of songs played) is saved on the computer's drive for later use.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> Computer P. <b>(ii)</b> Its Core i7 3.4 GHz 8-core processor can run audio-mixing and streaming software at the same time without lag. Its 16 GB of RAM lets large audio files and several programs stay in memory, so playback does not stutter on air. Its 1 TB SSD can hold the 45 GB library more than 20 times over and is fast and shock-resistant, and its six USB ports connect the mixing desk, microphones and backup drive. At $1,350 it is within the $1,500 budget. Computer Q's 64 GB storage could not hold the library and backups, and its single port is too few.</p><p><b>(c)(i)</b> 45 × 1,024 = <b>46,080 MB</b>. <b>(ii)</b> Advantage: a cloud copy is stored off-site, so if the studio drive fails again (or the building is damaged in a hurricane) the library can be restored and even played from another location. Disadvantage: the station depends on its Internet connection; if WaitukubuliTel's service is down, files cannot be reached, and large uploads/downloads are slow and there is a monthly subscription cost.</p><p><b>(d)(i)</b> Phone: a <b>length check</b> rejects any entry that is not exactly 7 characters (a type check could also allow digits only). Age: a <b>range check</b> accepts only ages between, for example, 10 and 100. <b>(ii)</b> <b>Validation</b> is an automatic check by the computer that data is sensible and follows set rules, but it cannot tell if the data is true. <b>Verification</b> checks that data has been entered or copied accurately from its source, for example by double entry or by proofreading against the original.</p><p><b>(e)</b> <b>Authority:</b> the post has no named author and comes from an account created yesterday, so the listener cannot tell who wrote it or whether they have any link to the station. They should check the station's official website or verified page. <b>Currency:</b> the post has no date, so even if a closure was once discussed, the information may be out of date. <b>Accuracy</b> could also be checked by comparing it with The Roseau Courier or the station's own announcements. The post fails these tests, so it is not reliable.</p><p><b>(f)</b> I recommend a 3-2-1 style strategy: three copies of the music library, on two kinds of storage, one off-site. <b>What:</b> the 45 GB music library, the playlists and the request log. <b>How often:</b> an automatic incremental backup every night after the last show, plus a full backup every Sunday. <b>Where:</b> (1) a second SSD or network drive (NAS) in the studio, mirrored with the main library, so Jahmal can switch to it within minutes if the main drive fails; (2) a cloud service such as an off-site storage provider, which protects against fire, theft or hurricane damage to the building. <b>Evaluation:</b> with a mirrored local copy, the two-hour outage would have been about two minutes, because Jahmal could simply point the playout software at the backup drive. The cloud copy adds protection the local copy cannot give. The limitations are the cost (a NAS plus a monthly fee) and the slow first upload of 45 GB on a typical connection, but after that only new songs are uploaded each night. On balance the strategy is affordable within the remaining budget and greatly reduces the risk of going off air.</p>",
