    "model": "<p><b>(a)(i)</b> The 20 office computers form a <b>LAN</b> (local area network). The network linking the office, media centre and four venues across the island is a <b>MAN</b> (metropolitan area network); WAN is also acceptable. <b>(ii)</b> The <b>switch</b> connects the computers within the LAN and forwards each message only to the computer it is meant for. The <b>router</b> connects the committee's LAN to other networks, such as the ISP and the Internet, and chooses the best path for data packets.</p><p><b>(b)</b> I recommend <b>fibre-optic cable</b>. It carries data as light, giving very high bandwidth for live HD video, and it can run the 3 km to the media centre without the signal weakening. It is also immune to electrical interference and hard to tap. <b>Twisted-pair</b> cable is cheaper but loses signal after about 100 m, has much lower bandwidth and suffers interference, so the video would stutter.</p><p><b>(c)(i)</b></p><pre>&lt;img src=\"cyg-logo.png\" alt=\"Caribbean Youth Games 2026 logo\"&gt;\n&lt;nav&gt;\n  &lt;a href=\"schedule.html\"&gt;Schedule&lt;/a&gt;\n  &lt;a href=\"results.html\"&gt;Results&lt;/a&gt;\n  &lt;a href=\"register.html\"&gt;Register&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"mailto:media@cyg2026.bs\"&gt;E-mail the media team&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"#venues\"&gt;Jump to Venues&lt;/a&gt;\n...\n&lt;h2 id=\"venues\"&gt;Venues&lt;/h2&gt;</pre><p><b>(ii)</b> A responsive layout that changes to a single column on narrow screens; large, well-spaced buttons that are easy to tap (also: compressed images for fast loading on mobile data, readable font sizes, a collapsible menu).</p><p><b>(d)(i)</b> An attacker sends athletes an e-mail or WhatsApp message that looks as if it comes from the Games committee, saying their registration is incomplete, with a link to a fake copy of the Register page. Athletes who type in their passport number and medical details hand them to the criminal, who can use them for identity theft, travel fraud or blackmail, and the athletes may lose money or their place at the Games. <b>(ii)</b> <b>Physical:</b> keep the server in a locked room with keycard or fingerprint entry and CCTV, so no one can steal or tamper with it. <b>Software:</b> encrypt the athlete database so stolen files cannot be read, and protect the network with a firewall that blocks unauthorised traffic. <b>Access privileges:</b> give each staff member a username and strong password (with 2FA) and allow only the medical team to view medical fields, so fewer people can leak or change them.</p><p><b>(e)</b> HTTPS encrypts all data sent between the athlete's browser and the web server, so passport and medical details cannot be read if intercepted (and the padlock shows the site's certificate).</p>",
    "key": "<p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a)(i) LAN (1); MAN or WAN (1). (ii) Switch function (1); router function (1).</li><li>(b) Fibre-optic recommended (1); two justified properties (2); comparison with another medium (1). Wireless/microwave accepted if well justified (max 3).</li><li>(c)(i) img with src (1) and alt (1); navigation hyperlink to a page (1); mailto link (1); #bookmark link with matching id/name target (1). Minor syntax slips: penalise once. (ii) Any two mobile-friendly features (2).</li><li>(d)(i) How phishing works (1), the deception/fake link (1), effect on athletes (1). (ii) Three countermeasures, 1 for naming and 1 for describing how it protects the data (6); must include one physical and one software measure, else max 4.</li><li>(e) Encryption (1).</li></ul>",
    "teacherTip": "<p>In (c) most lost marks are for the same-page link: the <code>href=\"#venues\"</code> needs a target with <code>id=\"venues\"</code>. In (d)(ii) candidates list \"password, antivirus, firewall\" with no explanation; each countermeasure needs HOW it protects the data. Physical means something you can touch.</p>",
