    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> E3: <code>=C3*D3</code> &nbsp; E8: <code>=SUM(E3:E7)</code></p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> G3: <code>=E3*(1-$F$1)</code> (or <code>=E3-E3*$F$1</code>). <b>(ii)</b> The discount rate is in one cell only. $F$1 is an absolute reference, so when the formula is copied down to G7 it still points to F1. A relative F1 would change to F2, F3 … and give wrong results.</p><p><b>(c)</b> F3: <code>=IF(D3&lt;20,\"Yes\",\"No\")</code> (copied down: Box of nails and Tarpaulin show Yes).</p><p><b>(d)</b> D9: <code>=MAX(D3:D7)</code>, which returns 260 (Hurricane strap).</p><p><b>(e)</b> A <b>column (or bar) chart</b>, because it compares separate items. It should have a chart title such as \"Bayside Hardware weekly sales by item\" and axis titles: \"Item\" (x-axis) and \"Sales ($)\" (y-axis). Data labels are also useful.</p><p><b>(f)(i)</b> Suppliers: primary key <b>SupplierID</b>. Products: primary key <b>Code</b>. <b>SupplierID</b> in Products is the foreign key. <b>(ii)</b> One-to-many: one supplier supplies many products. <b>(iii)</b> UnitPrice: Currency. InStock: Number (Integer), the quantity in stock.</p><p><b>(g)</b> Create a select query in Design View using BOTH tables, joined on SupplierID.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Field</th><td>Item</td><td>UnitPrice</td><td>SupplierName</td><td>Island</td></tr><tr><th>Table</th><td>Products</td><td>Products</td><td>Suppliers</td><td>Suppliers</td></tr><tr><th>Sort</th><td>Ascending</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Show</th><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>☐</td></tr><tr><th>Criteria</th><td></td><td>&gt;10</td><td></td><td>\"Trinidad\"</td></tr></table></div><p>Both criteria are on the same row, so they are joined with AND. SQL: <code>SELECT Item, UnitPrice, SupplierName FROM Products INNER JOIN Suppliers ON Products.SupplierID = Suppliers.SupplierID WHERE Island = \"Trinidad\" AND UnitPrice &gt; 10 ORDER BY Item;</code></p>",
    "key": "<p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) =C3*D3 (1); =SUM(E3:E7) (1). Missing = sign: penalise once in the question.</li><li>(b)(i) Correct calculation (1) with $F$1 (1). (ii) Reason (1).</li><li>(c) Condition D3&lt;20 (1); correct outputs in quotes in the right order (1).</li><li>(d) =MAX(D3:D7) (1).</li><li>(e) Column/bar (1); two of: chart title, axis titles, data labels (1).</li><li>(f)(i) SupplierID (1), Code (1), foreign key SupplierID in Products (1). (ii) One-to-many (1). (iii) Currency (1); Number (1).</li><li>(g) Both tables/join (1); correct fields (1); both criteria (1); sort by Item (1).</li></ul>",
    "teacherTip": "<p>The most common errors: forgetting the = sign, writing <code>=E3*$F$1</code> (that is the discount, not the discounted price) and putting text without quotation marks in IF. In (g), say BOTH tables and put the criteria on the SAME row for AND.</p>",
