     "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> E3: <code>=C3*D3</code> &nbsp; E8: <code>=SUM(E3:E7)</code></p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> G3: <code>=E3*(1-$F$1)</code> (or <code>=E3-E3*$F$1</code>). <b>(ii)</b> The discount rate is in one cell only. $F$1 is an absolute reference, so when the formula is copied down to G7 it still points to F1. A relative F1 would change to F2, F3 … and give wrong results.</p><p><b>(c)</b> F3: <code>=IF(D3&lt;20,\"Yes\",\"No\")</code> (copied down: Box of nails and Tarpaulin show Yes).</p><p><b>(d)</b> D9: <code>=MAX(D3:D7)</code>, which returns 260 (Hurricane strap).</p><p><b>(e)</b> A <b>column (or bar) chart</b>, because it compares separate items. It should have a chart title such as \"Bayside Hardware weekly sales by item\" and axis titles: \"Item\" (x-axis) and \"Sales ($)\" (y-axis). Data labels are also useful.</p><p><b>(f)(i)</b> Suppliers: primary key <b>SupplierID</b>. Products: primary key <b>Code</b>. <b>SupplierID</b> in Products is the foreign key. <b>(ii)</b> One-to-many: one supplier supplies many products. <b>(iii)</b> UnitPrice: Currency. InStock: Number (Integer), the quantity in stock.</p><p><b>(g)</b> Create a select query in Design View using BOTH tables, joined on SupplierID.</p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Field</th><td>Item</td><td>UnitPrice</td><td>SupplierName</td><td>Island</td></tr><tr><th>Table</th><td>Products</td><td>Products</td><td>Suppliers</td><td>Suppliers</td></tr><tr><th>Sort</th><td>Ascending</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><th>Show</th><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>☐</td></tr><tr><th>Criteria</th><td></td><td>&gt;10</td><td></td><td>\"Trinidad\"</td></tr></table></div><p>Both criteria are on the same row, so they are joined with AND. SQL: <code>SELECT Item, UnitPrice, SupplierName FROM Products INNER JOIN Suppliers ON Products.SupplierID = Suppliers.SupplierID WHERE Island = \"Trinidad\" AND UnitPrice &gt; 10 ORDER BY Item;</code></p><p><b>(h)(i)</b> H3: <code>=VLOOKUP(J1,$A$3:$C$7,3,FALSE)</code>. J1 is the code to find, A3:C7 is the table (absolute so it can be copied), 3 returns the third column (Unit price) and FALSE forces an exact match, so a mistyped code shows #N/A instead of a wrong price. <b>(ii)</b> First create a query using Suppliers and Products with the fields SupplierName, Item, UnitPrice, InStock and the calculated field <code>StockValue: [UnitPrice]*[InStock]</code>. Build the report from this query, <b>grouped on SupplierName</b>, sorted by Item within each group. In each SupplierName footer add a text box <code>=Sum([StockValue])</code> and a grand total in the report footer. Report title on two lines: <b>Bayside Hardware</b> / <b>Stock Value by Supplier</b>.</p>",
