     "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Data</b> is raw, unprocessed facts with no context, e.g. the number \"4\" written in the Number of people box. <b>Information</b> is data that has been processed so it has meaning and is useful, e.g. \"312 households in East End still need roofing\" in the weekly report.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> The paper registration form filled in door to door. <b>(ii)</b> The voucher is printed (output) by the computer with the household's barcode; the clerk then writes the items collected on it, and it is scanned back into the computer as input. A document that leaves the computer and returns as input is a turnaround document.</p><p><b>(c)</b> An <b>optical mark reader (OMR)</b>. The forms are printed with tick boxes in fixed positions; volunteers shade the boxes in dark ink, and the OMR shines light on each form to detect which positions are marked. It reads hundreds of forms quickly and avoids keying errors.</p><p><b>(d)(i)</b> Range check: only 1 to 20 accepted; rejects 0 or 25. <b>(ii)</b> Lookup (table) check against a list of valid districts (George Town, West Bay, East End, …); rejects \"Miami\". <b>(iii)</b> Presence check: the name must not be left blank; rejects an empty field. (A type check rejecting digits in a name is also acceptable.)</p><p><b>(e)</b> <b>Index-sequential</b>. Records are stored in household-number order, so the weekly report can read the file sequentially from start to end. An index lets the system jump straight to the one household when a voucher is scanned, giving fast direct access.</p><p><b>(f)</b> 1. Switch the laptop off, unplug it, remove the battery if possible and let it dry out completely before trying again, since flood water can short-circuit it. 2. Check the power and charger, then connect an external monitor through HDMI; if the picture appears there, the laptop's own screen or its cable is damaged. 3. Check that the vents and fan are not clogged with mud; if the fan stays loud or there is still no picture, back up the drive (remove it if needed) and send the laptop to a technician.</p><p><b>(g)(i)</b> Validation only checks that each entry obeys rules (range, presence, format). A household registered twice by two volunteers produces two records that are each perfectly sensible, so every check passes; validation cannot know that the same household already exists. <b>(ii)</b> <b>Verification:</b> the office clerk proofreads (visual check) each new form against the volunteer's photo of the household's ID card, and important fields such as address are entered by double entry, catching typing mistakes that would otherwise create 'new' households. <b>Design feature:</b> give each household a unique key based on an official identifier (e.g. the head of household's ID-card number) and set it as the primary key (Indexed: Yes, No Duplicates). The database will then refuse a second record with the same number, and the clerk is prompted to search for the existing household instead. Together these stop both deliberate double-claiming and accidental duplicates, so supplies reach more families fairly.</p>",
     "key": "<p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) Data with example (1); information with example (1).</li><li>(b)(i) Registration form (1). (ii) Output by computer (1), filled in and re-input (1).</li><li>(c) OMR (1); how it works (1); benefit (1). OCR earns 1 only if justified for the name/address boxes.</li><li>(d) For each field: suitable check (1) and valid rejected example (1).</li><li>(e) Index-sequential (1); direct access reason (1); sequential reason (1). Direct (random) alone earns max 2.</li><li>(f) 1 mark per sensible step; max 2 if the order is illogical or unsafe (e.g. opening the case while it is plugged in).</li><li>(g)(i) Explanation (1). (ii) Verification method (1) + justification (1); design feature (1) + justification (1).</li></ul>",
     "bloom": "Evaluate",
     "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> Data is … for example … Information is … for example …<br><b>(b)(i)</b> The … <b>(ii)</b> It is output by the computer, then … and then …<br><b>(c)</b> An OMR … because …<br><b>(d)(i)</b> Range check … rejects … <b>(ii)</b> … <b>(iii)</b> …<br><b>(e)</b> Index-sequential / direct because …<br><b>(f)</b> First … Next … Then …</p><p><b>Extension:</b> state your recommendation or design first, then give reasons, then weigh a limitation.</p>",
