    "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> First, 4G and 5G mobile data give enough bandwidth for live video consultations, so a patient in Anguilla can see a specialist in Barbados from home or the local clinic without travelling. Second, wearable devices such as smartwatches and glucose monitors connect by Bluetooth to the nurse's smartphone, and the readings are uploaded over mobile data, letting doctors monitor chronic patients continuously. (Also: SMS/app reminders, GPS for locating patients or ambulances.)</p><p><b>(b)</b> Weak or no mobile signal in hilly or remote areas, so video calls drop; the cost of a smartphone and a data plan, which some patients cannot afford.</p><p><b>(c)(i)</b> Phishing (spear phishing aimed at staff). <b>(ii)</b> <b>Patients:</b> private medical details such as HIV status or mental-health notes are exposed, which can cause embarrassment, discrimination at work and stress, and personal details can be used for identity fraud. <b>Organisation:</b> it loses the trust of patients and partner clinics, patients may leave, and it may face legal action or fines under data-protection law, plus the cost of investigating the breach. <b>(iii)</b> 1. Train all staff to recognise phishing e-mails (check the sender's address, never send passwords by e-mail, report suspicious messages). 2. Turn on multi-factor authentication for all staff accounts so that a stolen password on its own cannot be used to log in. (Also: e-mail filters that flag spoofed senders, access privileges, encryption of records.)</p><p><b>(d)(i)</b> Database administrator. <b>(ii)</b> Network administrator (or cybersecurity analyst).</p><p><b>(e)(i)</b> The <b>primary (main) document</b>: the appointment letter with merge fields such as «FirstName» and «AppointmentDate». The <b>data source</b>: the patient list containing names, addresses, island and appointment details. <b>(ii)</b> In Edit Recipient List, filter (or use a query) on the Island field so that Island equals \"St. Kitts\", then complete the merge; only matching records produce letters.</p><p><b>(f)</b> 1. Apply the built-in Heading 1 / Heading 2 styles to every chapter and section heading. 2. Insert the automatic table of contents (References ▸ Table of Contents) where it should appear, and use Update Table after editing.</p><p><b>(g)</b> Benefit: patients on small islands can see specialists quickly without the cost of flights and hotels. Drawback: the doctor cannot do a hands-on physical examination, and the service fails when Internet connections are poor (e.g. after a storm).</p>",
    "key": "<p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) 2 marks per way: technology named (1) + how it supports the service (1).</li><li>(b) 1 mark each.</li><li>(c)(i) Phishing (1). (ii) Patient impact (2: identify + develop); organisation impact (2). (iii) 2 marks per measure: named (1) + how it prevents a repeat (1). Antivirus earns 0.</li><li>(d) 1 mark each.</li><li>(e)(i) Each file with content (1 each). (ii) Filter/query (1) on the Island field = St. Kitts (1).</li><li>(f) Heading styles (1); insert/update TOC (1).</li><li>(g) 1 mark each.</li></ul>",
