     "teacherTip": "<p>In (a), the blanks are 5 (one pass per ballot) and c1 + 1; writing just 1 resets the count instead of adding to it. In (b), trace one ballot at a time: 1, 2, 2, 1, 2 gives c1 = 2 and c2 = 3. In (c), for i in range(5): repeats 5 times (range(1, 6) is also correct); range(1, 5) repeats only 4 times.</p>",
     "bloom": "Apply",
     "prompt": "<p>A simpler counter: exactly <b>5</b> ballots, each 1 or 2.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Fill in the blanks:<pre>c1 ← 0\nc2 ← 0\nFOR i ← 1 TO ___ DO\n   READ vote\n   IF vote = 1 THEN\n      c1 ← ___\n   ELSE\n      c2 ← c2 + 1\n   ENDIF\nENDFOR\nPRINT c1, c2</pre><i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Trace it with the ballots 1, 2, 2, 1, 2. What are c1 and c2 at the end? <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Write the Python <code>for</code> line that repeats 5 times. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p class=\"row\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/codelab.html?level=3\" data-id=\"codelab\">🐍 Open the Code Lab</button> <span class=\"hint\">Paste the pseudocode into the interpreter to check.</span></p>",
